  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Tuesday/...A low pressure system \nwill enter the Gulf of Alaska this weekend, bringing gale-force \nwinds to the outer coast along with periods of moderate to heavy \nrainfall associated with a weak atmospheric river. The initial \nwave of rain begins late Friday night into early Saturday morning \nas a front reaches the panhandle, impacting mainly the northern \nregion and more so the NE Gulf coast. A stronger wave then \nfollows, arriving by early Sunday morning. As the low slowly \ndissipates near south-central AK, it will meander and track \nmultiple fronts across the panhandle. This setup, reinforced by a \nconsistent slew of upper level troughs and divergent flow aloft, \nkeeps precipitation in the forecast for the extended period, \nalthough overall impacts are expected to diminish gradually \nheading into next week. One thing of note is as the low \ndissipates, the pressure gradient over the northern panhandle \nbecomes more north to south oriented, causing a potential increase\nin winds within Lynn Canal through Monday and Tuesday.\n\nImpacts associated with this weekend system will initially start \nalong the NE Gulf coast and as far south as Baranof Island, also \nupwind of higher elevations. From Sunday through Monday, heavier \nprecipitation will push south across most areas, particularly \ntargeting the Misty Fjords region. The highest confidence for 24-\nhour rainfall accumulations during Sunday's stronger wave ranges \nbetween 1.5 to 2.5 inches, with localized amounts reaching 4 to 5 \ninches over higher terrain. However, at this time there is a low \n(25%) chance that lower elevation areas surrounding Icy Strait, such \nas Juneau and Gustavus, could exceed 2.5 inches. A significant \nsoutherly jet will move along the coast Saturday, shifting \nsouthwesterly by Sunday morning and likely bringing 35 to 40 knot \nsustained winds from Cape Spencer north to Cape Suckling. Overnight \nSaturday into Sunday presents the greatest concern for elevated and \ngusty winds over the southern panhandle and inner channels, then \ntrending on the decline Monday morning.",
